Q: $match not matches all the feilds inputed A: <p>I have addressed the question, and here's my solution: I have implemented an expression within the pipeline to match the fields. I used the `$expr` and `$and` operators for this purpose.</p>
<pre><code>
    if (Object.keys(filter).length &gt; 0) {
      pipeline.push({ $match: { $expr: { $and: filter } } });
    }
</code></pre>

Q: How can I vertically center a div element for all browsers using CSS? A: <p>For newcomers, as this question is actual even now. But I hope this can help someone who finds it difficult doing this.<br />
Below are several reliable ways to vertically center a <strong><code>div</code> inside</strong> another <strong><code>div</code></strong> using modern and legacy CSS techniques.<br />
The examples use the same markup:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;child&quot;&gt;
    Centered content
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>We'll add <code>height: 300px</code> to <code>container</code>  to visually highlight the <code>child</code> position inside of it.</p>
<p><strong>1. Flexbox (Recommended modern approach)</strong></p>
<pre><code>.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal center */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical center */
  height: 300px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}

.child {
  padding: 20px;
  background: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. CSS Grid</strong></p>
<pre><code>.container {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* centers the child both vertically and horizontally */
  height: 300px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}

.child {
  padding: 20px;
  background: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. Absolute positioning + <code>transform: translateY</code></strong></p>
<pre><code>.container {
  position: relative;
  height: 300px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;      /* positions the top edge of the child halfway down the container */
  transform: translateY(-50%); /* shifts the element upward by half its own height */
  padding: 20px;
  background: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>4. <code>margin: auto</code> with absolute positioning</strong></p>
<pre><code>.container {
  position: relative;
  height: 300px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  margin: auto;
  height: 100px;
  width: 200px;
  background: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Setting both <code>top: 0</code> and <code>bottom: 0</code> allows vertical stretching.<br />
<code>margin: auto</code> distributes the remaining space equally, centering the element.<br />
<strong>NOTE:</strong> This method <strong>requires a fixed height</strong> for the child element.</p>
<p><strong>5. Table layout (older technique but also useful)</strong></p>
<p>This method is good because it works even in very old browsers.</p>
<pre><code>.container {
  display: table;
  height: 300px;
  width: 100%;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}

.child {
  display: table-cell;    /* allows vertical alignment */  
  vertical-align: middle; /* centers the content vertically */   
  text-align: center;
  padding: 20px;
  background: blue;
}
</code></pre>
